# Creating and Filing Plans

This guide assumes that you have read the primer at <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions>. The primer shows the create request. This guide explains each choice in that request.

## Before you create a plan

- **Edit, do not duplicate.** If a plan for the work exists, edit it. Plans keep their full history. If a new plan really replaces an old one, link the two plans and archive the old one.
- **Choose a folder.** Read `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/library` (see [Folders](#folders)).
- **Choose a type and read its template.** Read `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/plan_types` (see [Plan types](#plan-types)).

## Create request fields

`POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans` requires `title` and `content`. Optional fields:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `plan_type` | A type name. If you do not send one, the plan gets the type **General**. |
| `folder_path` | A path such as `"Team EBT/Q3"`. CoPlan creates missing folders in your principal's library. You can send `folder_id` instead. |
| `tags` | An array of tag names. CoPlan adds them to the type's `default_tags`. |
| `references` | An array of resources that the content does not link. See [References](#references). |
| `visibility` | Send `"draft"` only when your principal asks for a private plan. See [Visibility and archiving](#visibility-and-archiving). |

The response includes `id`, `url`, and `current_revision`.

## Plan types

Each plan has one type. A type has a description, default tags, and a content template. The type does not change how the plan renders. A `Presentation` plan is a normal plan whose template is a slide deck region (see <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/presentations>).

- Choose the most specific type that fits. Use **General** only after you check the list and no other type fits.
- The type's `template_content` is the structure that readers of that type expect. Keep its sections and fill them with real content. Remove a section only when it does not apply.
- CoPlan applies the type's `default_tags` when it creates the plan.
- If a plan changes into a different kind of document, change its type: `PATCH <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID` with `{"plan_type": "..."}`. Then restructure the content to match the new template. An unknown type name returns `422` with the list of valid names.
