## Folders

Each person has a library: a tree of folders, up to <%= CoPlan::Folder::MAX_DEPTH %> levels deep. A plan is filed in one folder in one library. Filing it in a different folder moves it. Use folders for where a plan lives, and use tags for what a plan is about.

- `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/library` returns your principal's folder tree. Each folder has a `description` of what belongs in it. Read the descriptions before you file a plan. Use the existing folders before you make new ones.
- To file or move a plan that your principal wrote, send `PATCH <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID` with `{"folder_path": "Team EBT/Q3"}`. To remove it from its folder, send `{"folder_id": ""}`.
- Name folders like places (`Team EBT`, `Infra`, `Q3 Launch`). Give each new folder a one-line description. The next agent reads it.
- Folder endpoints: `GET`, `POST`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE` on `<%= @base %>/api/v1/folders`. You can delete only empty folders.

To reorganize a library, or to move many plans, fetch <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/organizing>.

## Tags

- Read `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/tags` before you add a tag. It lists the tags in use, most used first. Use an existing tag when one fits.
- Use lowercase names with hyphens: `api-design`, `cost-reduction`.
- Use 1 to 3 tags for each plan. A tag names a domain or concern (`security`, `onboarding`), not a status or a team.
- To set tags, send `PATCH <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID` with `{"tags": ["security", "api-design"]}`. This replaces the full tag list.

## References

References are the list of resources connected to a plan: repositories, pull requests, documents, and other plans. Readers see them in the plan's References section. CoPlan adds every link in the content to this list. Add a reference through the API only for a resource that the content does not link.

```bash
<%= @curl %> -X POST \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"url": "https://github.com/org/auth-service", "key": "auth-repo", "title": "Auth Service"}' \
  "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/references" | jq .
```

- `url` is required. Always set `key`, a short stable name such as `auth-repo` or `impl-pr` (lowercase letters, numbers, `-`, and `_`; unique in the plan). Set `title` to a name that people can read, not the URL.
- CoPlan sets the type (repository, pull request, plan, document, or link) from the URL.
- Only the plan author can add or remove references through the API. You can also send `references` on create or in a `PATCH` to the plan.
- `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/references` lists the references of a plan.
- `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/references/search?url=...` finds the plans that refer to a URL.

## Visibility and archiving

A plan has two independent settings. There is no status. Do not put a status in the document.

| Field | Values | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `visibility` | `published`, `draft` | `published` plans are listed for the whole organization. This is the default. A `draft` plan shows as **private**: it is not in lists, search, or libraries, but anyone with the URL can read it. |
| `archived` | `true`, `false` | Archived plans stay at their URL but are not in lists or search. |

- Create a private plan only when your principal asks for one. To share it later, send `PATCH` with `{"visibility": "published"}`. Through the API, you cannot make a shared plan private again. Archive it instead.
- Archive plans that are finished, replaced, or created by mistake, including your own duplicates: `PATCH` with `{"archived": true}`. To restore a plan, send `{"archived": false}`.
- To show a plan's rollout stage, use a tag such as `developing` or `live`.
- The API still accepts the old `status` field (`brainstorm`, `considering`, `developing`, `live`, `abandoned`) and converts it to `visibility` and `archived`. Do not use it in new code.
